{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent negative bias: model under-forecasts levels throughout the horizon, especially during rallies. Apply an upward level shift (roughly +1 to +4 €/t early, rising to +3 to +6 €/t mid/late horizon when trend is up).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: model reacts too slowly to upward momentum; true series ramps faster. Add a momentum term based on recent 5–10d slope (if slope > 0, boost forecast increasingly with horizon).",
    "Horizon-dependent underestimation: errors grow with lead time (small at days 1–3, larger from ~day 7 onward). Use a lead-time scaling factor on the correction (e.g., minimal adjustment near-term, larger adjustment mid/long-term).",
    "Volatility compression: model forecasts are too smooth and miss breakout amplitude. Increase forecast variance during strong-trend regimes (e.g., when last 10d range or ATR is elevated).",
    "Regime misclassification after local dips: when history shows a drawdown then stabilization, model assumes continued weakness/mean reversion, but true outcomes often reverse into a sustained uptrend. If price is below recent 10–20d average but slope turns positive, flip to bullish correction rather than mean-reversion down."
  ]
}
